<subsection xml:id="subsec-higher-order-introduction">
  <title>Linear ODEs of Higher Order</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In Section 3-1 we developed systematic solution methods for first-order
      ODEs.  Many physical and engineering phenomena, however, require
      higher-order equations.
      A vibrating spring, the bending of a structural beam, or the current
      in an RLC circuit all give rise naturally to <em>second-order</em> (or
      higher) differential equations.
    </p>
    <p>
      This section focuses on the most tractable and practically important
      subclass: <em>linear ODEs with constant coefficients</em>.
      For this class a complete algorithmic solution procedure exists, based
      on factoring a polynomial (the <em>characteristic polynomial</em>) and
      reading off the solutions from its roots.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ─── Engineering motivation ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-higher-order-motivation">
    <title>Higher-Order ODEs in Engineering</title>
    <p>
      Two classical engineering models immediately lead to higher-order linear
      ODEs with constant coefficients.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          <em>Harmonic oscillator (mass-spring-damper).</em>
          A mass <m>m</m> attached to a spring of stiffness <m>k</m> and a
          damper of coefficient <m>\alpha</m> satisfies
          <me>
            m\, y''(t) + \alpha\, y'(t) + k\, y(t) = F(t),
          </me>
          where <m>y(t)</m> is the displacement from rest and <m>F(t)</m>
          is an external force.
          This is a second-order linear ODE with constant coefficients.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <em>Elastic beam bending.</em>
          Under a distributed transverse load <m>q(x)</m>, the deflection
          <m>w(x)</m> of a homogeneous beam with bending stiffness
          <m>EI</m> satisfies
          <me>
            E\, I\, w''''(x) = q(x).
          </me>
          This is a <em>fourth-order</em> linear ODE.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <figure xml:id="fig-higher-order-mass-spring-visual">
      <caption>
        A mass-spring-damper model.  The restoring spring force, damping force,
        and external input combine to produce a second-order equation for the
        displacement <m>y(t)</m>.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-higher-order-mass-spring-visual" width="72%">
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}[
            scale=0.95,
            spring/.style={decorate,
              decoration={coil, aspect=0.45, segment length=5pt,
                          amplitude=5pt}},
            axis/.style={->, thick, gray!70},
            force/.style={->, very thick, red!75!black},
            every node/.style={font=\small}
          ]
            \begin{scope}[shift={(-2.35,0)}]
              % Fixed support
              \fill[gray!25] (-1.25,2.65) rectangle (1.25,2.9);
              \foreach \x in {-1.15,-0.85,...,1.15}
                \draw[gray!55] (\x,2.65) -- (\x+0.18,2.9);
              \draw[thick] (-1.25,2.65) -- (1.25,2.65);

              % Spring above the liquid cylinder
              \draw[spring, thick] (0,2.65) -- (0,1.05);
              \node[right] at (0.22,1.88) {$k$};
              \draw[thick] (0,1.05) -- (0,0.62);

              % Cylinder with viscous liquid
              \fill[cyan!18] (-0.95,-1.25) rectangle (0.95,0.82);
              \draw[cyan!55!black, thick] (-0.95,0.82) -- (-0.95,-1.25);
              \draw[cyan!55!black, thick] (0.95,0.82) -- (0.95,-1.25);
              \draw[cyan!55!black, thick] (-0.95,-1.25)
                arc[start angle=180,end angle=360,x radius=0.95,y radius=0.18];
              \draw[cyan!55!black, thick] (-0.95,0.82)
                arc[start angle=180,end angle=360,x radius=0.95,y radius=0.18];
              \draw[cyan!55!black, dashed] (0.95,0.82)
                arc[start angle=0,end angle=180,x radius=0.95,y radius=0.18];
              \node[cyan!45!black] at (0,-0.95) {viscous liquid};
              \node[cyan!45!black, right] at (0.95,-0.2) {$\alpha$};

              % Mass moving inside the liquid
              \fill[blue!20] (-0.48,-0.05) rectangle (0.48,0.58);
              \draw[very thick, blue!65!black] (-0.48,-0.05) rectangle (0.48,0.58);
              \node at (0,0.26) {$m$};

              % External force and displacement
              \draw[force] (0.6,0.26) -- (0.6,-0.75)
                node[midway, right] {$F(t)$};
              \draw[dashed, gray!70] (-1.2,0.26) -- (1.25,0.26);
              \node[left, gray!70] at (-1.2,0.26) {rest};
              \draw[axis] (1.35,0.82) -- (1.35,-1.25) node[below] {$y$};
              \draw[->, thick, blue!70!black] (1.6,0.26) -- (1.6,-0.65)
                node[midway, right] {$y(t)$};
            \end{scope}

            \begin{scope}[shift={(3.15,0.15)}]
              \draw[axis] (0,0) -- (3.15,0) node[right] {$t$};
              \draw[axis] (0,-0.75) -- (0,1.25) node[above] {$y(t)$};
              \draw[blue!65!black, very thick, domain=0:3.0, samples=120]
                plot (\x,{0.95*exp(-0.55*\x)*cos(5.2*\x r)});
              \draw[dashed, gray!55] (0,0) -- (3.05,0);
              \node[align=center] at (1.55,-1.08)
                {typical damped\\response};
            \end{scope}
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <figure xml:id="fig-higher-order-beam-visual">
      <caption>
        Beam bending under a distributed load.  The fourth derivative appears
        because curvature and internal bending moments must be differentiated
        to balance the transverse load.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-higher-order-beam-visual" width="78%">
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}[
            scale=0.95,
            load/.style={->, very thick, red!75!black},
            beam/.style={line width=4pt, blue!65!black},
            every node/.style={font=\small}
          ]
            \draw[beam] (-3.7,0) -- (3.7,0);
            \draw[blue!35, line width=7pt, opacity=0.35]
              plot[smooth, domain=-3.7:3.7, samples=120]
              (\x, {-0.43*(1-(\x/3.7)^2)});
            \draw[blue!70!black, very thick]
              plot[smooth, domain=-3.7:3.7, samples=120]
              (\x, {-0.43*(1-(\x/3.7)^2)});

            \foreach \x in {-3.1,-2.45,...,3.1}
              \draw[load] (\x,1.55) -- (\x,0.22);
            \draw[red!75!black, thick] (-3.35,1.55) -- (3.35,1.55);
            \node[red!75!black, above] at (0,1.65) {distributed load $q(x)$};

            \draw[thick] (-3.7,0) -- (-4.05,-0.75) -- (-3.35,-0.75) -- cycle;
            \draw[thick] (3.7,0) -- (3.35,-0.75) -- (4.05,-0.75) -- cycle;
            \draw[gray!70] (-4.25,-0.75) -- (-3.15,-0.75);
            \draw[gray!70] (3.15,-0.75) -- (4.25,-0.75);

            \draw[->, thick, gray!70] (-4.25,-1.15) -- (4.25,-1.15)
              node[right] {$x$};
            \draw[dashed, gray!65] (0,0) -- (0,-0.43);
            \draw[latex-latex, thick, green!45!black] (0,0) -- (0,-0.43)
              node[midway, right] {$w(x)$};
            \node[blue!65!black, below] at (0,-0.88) {deflected shape};
            \node[above, blue!65!black] at (2.85,0.18) {$EI$};
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>
    <p>
      Both examples share the structural feature we will exploit: the unknown
      function and all its derivatives appear only <em>linearly</em>, and the
      coefficients are <em>constant</em>.
    </p>
  </example>

  <!-- ─── Definition: Linear ODE of order n ─── -->

  <definition xml:id="def-linear-ode-order-n">
    <title>Linear ODE of Order <m>n</m></title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        An ODE of order <m>n</m> is <term>linear</term> if it can be written as
        <me>
          y^{(n)}(t) + a_{n-1}(t)\, y^{(n-1)}(t) + \cdots
          + a_1(t)\, y'(t) + a_0(t)\, y(t) = f(t),
        </me>
        where the <term>coefficient functions</term> <m>a_0(t), \ldots, a_{n-1}(t)</m>
        and the <term>right-hand side</term> <m>f(t)</m> are given.
        The unknown function <m>y(t)</m> and its derivatives appear only to the
        first power and are never multiplied together or nested inside nonlinear
        functions.
      </p>
      <p>
        Important special cases:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Constant coefficients:</term>
              all <m>a_i(t) = a_i</m> are constants.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Homogeneous:</term> <m>f(t) = 0</m> for all <m>t</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <term>Inhomogeneous:</term> <m>f(t) \not\equiv 0</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <remark xml:id="rem-linearity-meaning">
    <title>What Linearity Rules Out</title>
    <p>
      The linearity condition says that <m>y</m> never appears inside a
      nonlinear expression.  Terms such as <m>y^2(t)</m>, <m>\sin(y(t))</m>,
      <m>\sqrt{y(t)}</m>, or <m>y(t)\, y'(t)</m> all violate linearity.
      By contrast, coefficients like <m>\sin(t)</m> or <m>e^{t}</m>
      are fine, because they depend only on the <em>independent variable</em>
      <m>t</m>, not on the unknown <m>y</m>.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <!-- ─── Classification example ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-higher-order-classification">
    <title>Classifying Higher-Order ODEs</title>
    <p>
      Determine for each ODE: is it linear?  If so, is it homogeneous?
      Does it have constant coefficients?
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>4\, y'''(t) + e^2\, y''(t) + \sin(t)\, y'(t) + \sqrt{y(t)} = 0.</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>y''''(t) + e^2\, y''(t) + \pi\, y'(t) + y(t) = e^{-t}.</m>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>y'''(t) - \sin(2t)\, y''(t) + \pi\, y'(t) + y(t) = 0.</m>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Classification.</em>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <em>Not linear:</em> the term <m>\sqrt{y(t)}</m> is a nonlinear
            function of <m>y</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <em>Linear, not homogeneous, constant coefficients:</em>
            all derivatives of <m>y</m> appear linearly; <m>f(t) = e^{-t} \neq 0</m>;
            the coefficients <m>1, e^2, \pi, 1</m> do not depend on <m>t</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <em>Linear, homogeneous, non-constant coefficients:</em>
            <m>f(t) = 0</m>, but the coefficient <m>-\sin(2t)</m> of
            <m>y''</m> depends on <m>t</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
    <p>
      The solution methods developed in this section apply to cases like (2):
      linear, with constant coefficients.
    </p>
  </example>

  <!-- ─── Overview figure: classification summary ─── -->

  <figure xml:id="fig-higher-order-classification-tree">
    <caption>
      Classification of higher-order ODEs.  This section treats the shaded
      class: linear, constant-coefficient ODEs — the most algorithmically
      accessible family.
    </caption>
    <image xml:id="image-higher-order-classification-tree" width="70%">
      <latex-image>
        \begin{tikzpicture}[
          node distance=1.1cm and 2.2cm,
          box/.style={draw, rounded corners, minimum width=3.2cm,
                      minimum height=0.75cm, align=center, font=\small},
          shade/.style={fill=blue!12},
          arr/.style={->, thick}
        ]
          \node[box] (all) {All ODEs of order \(n\)};
          \node[box, below left=of all]  (nonlin) {Nonlinear};
          \node[box, below right=of all] (lin)    {Linear};
          \node[box, below left=of lin]  (vcoef)  {Variable\\coefficients};
          \node[box, below right=of lin, shade] (ccoef)
              {Constant\\coefficients};
          \node[box, below left=of ccoef]  (inhom) {Inhomogeneous};
          \node[box, below right=of ccoef] (hom)   {Homogeneous};
          \draw[arr] (all) -- (nonlin);
          \draw[arr] (all) -- (lin);
          \draw[arr] (lin) -- (vcoef);
          \draw[arr] (lin) -- (ccoef);
          \draw[arr] (ccoef) -- (inhom);
          \draw[arr] (ccoef) -- (hom);
        \end{tikzpicture}
      </latex-image>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-higher-order-introduction-check">
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-higher-order-classify-equation">
      <title>Classify the Equation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider
          <me>
            y'''-2y''+y=\cos t.
          </me>
          Which description is correct?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>Third-order, linear, inhomogeneous, with constant coefficients</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Correct. The coefficients of <m>y''', y'', y</m> are constants, and the right-hand side is nonzero.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Third-order, nonlinear, homogeneous</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. The equation is linear, and it is not homogeneous because of <m>\cos t</m>.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Second-order, linear, homogeneous</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. The highest derivative is third order.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>First-order, nonlinear, with variable coefficients</p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. None of those labels fits this equation.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-higher-order-associated-homogeneous">
      <title>Associated Homogeneous Equation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Write down the associated homogeneous equation for
          <me>
            y''+3y'=e^t.
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The associated homogeneous equation is obtained by replacing the right-hand side by <m>0</m>:
          <me>
            y''+3y'=0.
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>

</subsection>
